  "title": "Climate threats to rainforest life",
  "refs": [
   "3.5b"
  ],
  "minutes": 25,
  "cards": [
   [
    "Heat and rainfall changes stress a living system",
    "Warming can increase evaporative demand; longer or more intense dry periods can reduce soil water and stress trees. Stomata may close to limit water loss, also restricting carbon dioxide uptake and photosynthesis. Extreme heat can further limit growth. Changes vary between forests and seasons; one dry day or one local drought does not by itself establish a long-term climate trend."
   ],
   [
    "Structure, functioning and biodiversity are linked",
    "Tree deaths and altered regeneration can open the canopy and change shade, temperature and moisture below. Nutrient cycling, carbon storage, water transfer and food supply may change. Species differ in their tolerances, mobility and dependence on partners, so some populations decline or shift. Climate pressure interacts with fragmentation, ignition and land use; hot dry conditions raise fire risk but do not explain every ignition."
   ],
   [
    "Feedbacks change risks; outcomes are not fixed dates",
    "Loss of trees can reduce water recycling and release stored carbon through decay or burning, reinforcing drying or warming. Higher carbon dioxide can affect photosynthesis, but nutrients, heat and water constraints mean it cannot be assumed to cancel damage. Projections depend on future emissions, land use and forest responses. A single universal temperature threshold or collapse date for all rainforests is not justified by this teaching model."
   ]
  ],
